\section{Price a Revision That Invalidates Only Once}

A guard miss that retires an artifact changes the value of all
later entries. Use a truncated model in which, after installation,
at most $N$ entries remain, each eligible entry independently
invalidates the version with probability $q$, and the first
invalidation permanently returns to baseline. The first miss incurs
an extra cost $b$, versus baseline, and never receives the fast
saving $\delta$. Every prior success receives $\delta$. Costs after
retirement match baseline. This is a hypothetical policy model,
not a claim that actual program phases are independent. Use
one consistent time unit and count installation and compilation
once.

\begin{sudocode}[Accumulate expected benefit while the version is live]
\State $live\gets 1$; $gain\gets -C-P$
\For{$i\gets 1$ to $N$}
  \If{$live=0$}
    \State \textbf{break} \Comment{The version is certainly retired}
  \EndIf
  \State $miss\gets live\cdot q$
  \State $hit\gets live\cdot(1-q)$
  \State $gain\gets gain+hit\cdot\delta$
    \Comment{Only a hit earns the saving}
  \State $gain\gets gain-miss\cdot b$
    \Comment{Charge only the first invalidation}
  \State $live\gets hit$
\EndFor
\State \Return $gain$
\end{sudocode}

Take $N=3$, $q=1/2$, savings 8 per hit, a miss surcharge
4, and a combined fixed cost of 5. The hit probabilities are
$1/2,1/4,1/8$ and sum to $7/8$; so do the miss probabilities
in this particular case. Net expected saving is
$7-3.5-5=-1.5$, and a zero-margin policy declines the job.
Treating every entry as eligible after a miss would predict
$3(4-2)-5=1$ and enqueue it. Set $q=0$ as a second test:
the answer is $3\cdot 8-5=19$. If $N=0$, only the fixed
cost is charged.

\Cref{fig:visual-257} makes the shrinking probability of another optimized entry explicit. A miss removes the remainder of that run from the calculation.

